WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680
Welcome to Fascinating Frontiers Episode 142 for July 25th, 2026.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.180
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:14.980
Astronomers report the first strong evidence for an exo-moon orbiting a planet 73 light years away.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.660
Space X's Starship launched from Starbase, Texas, deployed 20 next-generation Starlink V3 satellites

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.940
and achieved a controlled splashdown in the Indian Ocean.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:35.360
The Starship Upper Stage successfully released an initial batch of the larger, more capable Starlink V3 spacecraft during the flight test.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.340
These satellites feature upgraded power and communication systems compared with earlier versions.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.000
Deployment marks the first operational use of the new Starlink generation on Starship.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:53.580
The satellites were placed into their target orbits after the upper stage completed its primary burn sequence.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:01.180
The next-generation spacecraft bring expanded onboard systems that support greater data throughput once operational.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:07.560
This deployment integrates the new satellite design directly into the ongoing constellation refresh.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:15.060
The vehicle performed a banking maneuver that rehearses the approach profile needed for future tower catches at Starbase.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:23.340
This flight addressed issues seen on the previous test and returned extensive data on thermal protection and propulsion performance.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:31.760
The upper stage re-entered Earth's atmosphere ahead of the Indian Ocean landing while the booster executed its own recovery sequence.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.480
Remember the show covered Starship development on EP-141.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:44.560
Today's test moves the program forward on the open questions of rapid reuse cadence and orbital refueling demonstrations.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:53.620
While Starship pushes hardware boundaries, astronomers are using existing telescopes to push the boundaries of what we can detect around other stars.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:03.720
Astronomers using the very large telescope in Chile observed repeated gravitational tugs on a planet orbiting the star CD-352722.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:12.780
The companion object has a mass close to Jupiter's yet follows an orbit that challenges standard formation models for both planets and moons.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:19.820
The detection relies on transit timing variations measured across multiple epochs rather than direct imaging.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:28.960
The system lies in a region where the host stars properties differ markedly from the sun, adding another layer of complexity to the interpretation.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:35.940
Follow-up observations with additional facilities are planned to confirm the signal and refine the mass estimate.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:41.840
To understand how such a signal is extracted, here's a quick look at the technique itself.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:53.960
If Earth's year were compressed into a single hour on a clock, the gravitational tug from a moon the size of Jupiter would shift the planet's arrival time by several minutes across successive orbits.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:04.500
That tiny delay accumulates because the moon and planet orbit their common center of mass so the planet does not cross its star at perfectly regular intervals.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:13.240
Astronomers measure these cumulative offsets over many transits with ground-based spectrographs that time the events to within seconds.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:23.360
At 73 light years, the signal is detectable only because the moon's mass is large enough to pull its planet by tens of thousands of kilometers during each orbit.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:35.560
The same technique has already uncovered dozens of non-transiting planets yet applying it to moons remains rare because the timing amplitude is smaller and requires years of continuous monitoring.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:48.100
We can now see the effect clearly in the CD-352722 system, but nobody yet knows whether the moon formed in place or was captured after the planet migrated inward.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:57.820
From one surprising detection to another, researchers have also turned the event horizon telescope array back on the famous black hole in M87.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:11.920
Researchers combine 2018 event horizon telescope and global millimeter VLBI array data to produce the first spatially resolved dual frequency study of the M87 star accretion flow.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:18.760
The images reveal temperature and density gradients across the inner disk that were previously unresolved.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:24.200
The work shows how synchrotron emission changes with frequency near the event horizon.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:30.680
The dual frequency approach isolates regions where magnetic fields and particle energies shift rapidly.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:37.220
Additional multi-frequency campaigns are expected to map magnetic field structure in greater detail.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:43.640
While some teams chase the extreme, others simply watch the familiar sky change with the seasons.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:56.300
On July 25th, the Great Square Asterism, formed by Alganib, Markub, Sheet and Alpharats, becomes prominent in the eastern sky after 10.30pm local daylight time.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.800
Only three of the four stars belong to Pegasus. Alpharats is the brightest star in Andromeda.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:09.220
Observers can use the square to locate other autumn constellations as they climb higher through the night.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:17.020
The Moon's passage near Antares earlier the same evening provides an additional reference point for locating the rising square.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:23.720
Back on the exploration front, NASA has named the target region for its next crude lunar landing.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:33.120
NASA's Artemis 4 mission plans to land astronauts in the Moon's polar region to study water ice deposits and test resource utilization.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:39.000
The site selection supports long-term surface operations and future gateway rendezvous.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.920
The polar terrain offers access to permanently shadowed creators where ice may remain stable.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.340
Remember, the show covered Artemis on EP 141.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.460
This announcement advances planning for crude landing schedules and lander readiness.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.980
NASA leadership is also publicly tying that future to one specific vehicle.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:09.340
NASA's chief highlighted starships potential to transform access to the Moon and Mars through its large payload capacity and reusability.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:16.980
The comments tied directly to ongoing collaboration between NASA and SpaceX on Artemis lunar lander development.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:23.100
The administrator noted the vehicle's ability to deliver substantial hardware in a single flight.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.280
Remember, the show covered starship on EP 141.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:31.860
Today's statement underscores the vehicle's role in the crude Mars timeline.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:38.300
Meanwhile, a smaller but still notable discovery has astronomers rethinking formation models again.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:45.140
A body with nearly Jupiter's mass was detected circling a brown dwarf 71 light years from Earth.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:53.020
The object sits at the boundary between planet and star classifications and does not fit standard formation scenarios.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:00.040
The discovery, published in Nature, raises questions about how such companions form around substellar hosts.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:07.500
The system challenges existing definitions because the central body lacks sufficient mass to sustain hydrogen fusion.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:14.100
On a more practical note, regional industry is quietly supporting the broader space economy.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:21.700
A company in North Texas supplies components and services that contribute to the expanding private space sector in the state.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:30.060
Its work supports both launch vehicle development and satellite manufacturing efforts centered around the growing Texas aerospace corridor.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:37.860
The firm's contributions help sustain supply chains from multiple commercial and government programs operating from the region.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.500
Farther afield, modeling work continues on another planet's climate.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:52.400
The Mars Climate Modeling Center at NASA Ames runs global climate simulations to study dust, water, and carbon dioxide cycles on the red planet.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:59.080
The models help interpret data from current orbiters and rovers while preparing for future landed missions.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:06.300
Outputs feed into long-term climate history reconstructions that compare present conditions with ancient epochs.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:14.120
The center's work integrates observations from multiple spacecraft to refine predictions of seasonal and regional weather patterns.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:18.860
One of the networks that keeps those missions talking is itself under threat.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:26.240
One of NASA's key antennas used for communicating with distant spacecraft is threatened by wildfire activity.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:32.920
Loss of the facility would reduce tracking and data return capacity for multiple ongoing missions.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:40.880
Engineers are assessing protective measures and backup options that could maintain contact with probes at the edge of the solar system.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:49.040
The antenna forms part of the network that relays commands and science data from Mars, outer planet, and heliophysics missions.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:54.840
While hardware faces earthly hazards, NASA continues to fund foundational research.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:09:04.260
Amendment 66 to Roses 2025 confirms the due date for step two proposals under the Heliosyfix foundational research program.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:10.720
The solicitation fund studies of Sun Heliosphere planetary interactions across scales.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:19.200
Selected investigations will address fundamental drivers of space weather and plasma processes that affect both Earth and other planets.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:27.100
Proposals must demonstrate how the work advances understanding of energy transfer from the solar interior to planetary environments.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:32.420
The agency is also asking the community how it might run future prize competitions.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:40.120
The agency released a request seeking community input on the structure and focus of a potential new prize competition.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:47.500
Responses will help shape challenge areas that could accelerate technology development for future space science missions.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:56.460
The request invites ideas on prize topics, eligibility rules, and evaluation criteria that align with NASA's science priorities.

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:02.380
Input gathered will inform whether a new competition is launched and how it might be administered.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:10.200
Keep an eye on follow up observations that could confirm the exomune candidate around CD352722.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:13.060
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

